From 78213adade565094c320a3c9a5c88dc711b7540e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aamir <aamir@tallow-lang.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:57:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/27] test+interp: parity for StructuredData.extract, and the
 defect it found
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The three graded assertions the class uses (both legs exit 0 with the native leg refusing
to fall back; stdout byte-identical; stdout equal to a pinned reference) all pass on the
four deliberately-discriminating documents, so the P2.T1 binding is now proven against
the interpreter rather than against itself.

The fourth assertion did NOT pass, and that is the point of it. `extract(42)`:

  * the interpreter exit 0 and printed `false` — its arm coerced the argument with
    `v.as_string()`, parsed `"42"` as HTML, and returned a plausible `has_json_ld: false`;
  * the compiled leg refused, because its arm requires an I8Ptr and has no coercion to
    offer;
  * `tallow check` PASSED on the bad argument (1 items, 1 warning), so nothing caught it
    before run time either.

That is the "plausible success, wrong answer" class this harness exists to catch, and it
means T4's requirement — the same refusal from both engines — was unmet by the
interpreter, not by the binding.

Fix: the interpreter arm now matches `Value::String` and refuses anything else, naming the
same cause as the compiled leg. The two agree on the phrase
"expected an HTML string argument"; the compiled leg refuses at LOWERING time and the
interpreter at run time, so the *kind* of failure differs while the cause does not, and the
test asserts the cause.

Two limits recorded in the test rather than left implied:

  * refusing at run time is a floor, not the fix. The declared `html: String` is not
    enforced by the type checker — `tallow check` accepts the bad call — so the real fix
    belongs there.
  * `v.as_string()` appears 549 times in `interp.rs`. Coercing arguments is the
    interpreter's house convention, so this arm is deliberately the strict one; whether
    the other 548 should behave the same is a language-level question that needs its own
    audit, not a patch smuggled in under a binding.

Verified: cargo test --test structured_data_parity → pass (both tests); the FFI suite and
the cli suite re-run green after the interpreter change; and the fixtures were
deliberately built with no braces in any HTML literal, because Tallow reads `{...}` inside
a string as interpolation — which is how the first version of the probe broke the native
build and made --require-native refuse.
---
